<BaseLayout
  title="Deployment and Integration"
  description="BeaconGuard deployment and integration using three contracts—request and identity, provider-result correlation, and intended enterprise action—with bypass prohibitions on outbound and return paths."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Deployment and Integration</h1>

    <p>
      This section describes how BeaconGuard is deployed and integrated into
      existing systems. Guidance is conservative and environment-agnostic for
      regulated enterprise settings under <strong>controlled internal
      qualification</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard deploys alongside existing applications. Applications submit
      trusted request and action context, enforce returned <strong>ALLOW</strong> or
      <strong>DENY</strong> results, and rely on Verifiable Decision Records for
      governance review. Deployment responsibilities are shared with the customer.
    </p>

    <DeploymentTopology />

    <h2>Deployment Model</h2>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard places runtime authorization on the path to the AI provider and
      on the return / declared-action path where enforcement is configured. An
      optional <strong>System Connector</strong> may map source protocols into the
      required request format. The Control Plane (Govern) operates outside the
      per-request path. BeaconGuard Verify is a read-only functional domain.
    </p>

    <p>Common characteristics:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Standalone process or container on the runtime authorization path</li>
      <li>Deterministic evaluation against an Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>Externalized configuration and secrets</li>
      <li>Deployment-dependent scale characteristics (no universal horizontal-scaling or multi-region claim)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The deployment environment (customer) is responsible for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Process isolation and host hardening</li>
      <li>Network controls that keep BeaconGuard on outbound and return paths</li>
      <li>Transport security (for example TLS)</li>
      <li>Secrets and signing-key management</li>
      <li>Identity provider integration</li>
      <li>Evidence-provider configuration and monitoring</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Three Integration Contracts</h2>

    <h3>1. Request and identity contract</h3>
    <p>
      The enterprise application (or System Connector) supplies trusted request
      context and identity/tenant attributes required for Request Admission
      Authorization. Missing or malformed required inputs fail closed as DENY.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Provider-result and transaction-correlation contract</h3>
    <p>
      After admission ALLOW, the application may invoke the AI provider. The
      returned AI-generated result must be bound to governed transaction context
      with correlation identifiers that relate request and later action
      decisions. Provider success is not BeaconGuard authorization.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Intended enterprise-action contract</h3>
    <p>
      The enterprise application declares the Intended Enterprise Action and
      required context for Result-to-Action Authorization. Only after action
      ALLOW may the application execute the authorized action into the enterprise
      system of record.
    </p>

    <h2>Bypass Prohibitions</h2>

    <p>An application must not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Invoke the provider without required request authorization</li>
      <li>Apply a provider result without required result/action authorization</li>
      <li>Reuse prior authority in place of a new governed authorization</li>
      <li>Treat provider success as BeaconGuard authorization</li>
      <li>Allow report or AI-insight output into the runtime permission path</li>
      <li>Bypass BeaconGuard on the return path</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Integration Pattern</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>Application (or System Connector) prepares trusted request context</li>
      <li>Request Admission Authorization evaluates against the Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>Verifiable Decision Record is persisted; DENY hard-stops with no provider invocation</li>
      <li>On ALLOW, application invokes the AI provider</li>
      <li>AI-generated result is bound to transaction context (untrusted proposal)</li>
      <li>Application declares Intended Enterprise Action</li>
      <li>Result-to-Action Authorization evaluates; Verifiable Decision Record is persisted</li>
      <li>DENY hard-stops with no enterprise effect; ALLOW permits application execution into the system of record</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Where enforcement requires it, evidence is recorded before forward or before
      enterprise effect. Exact ordering is mode- and configuration-dependent.
      Timeout and retry behavior are deployment-specific; do not invent combined
      dual-boundary latency figures.
    </p>

    <p>BeaconGuard does not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Call back into applications to execute workflow steps</li>
      <li>Replace workflow logic or systems of record</li>
      <li>Treat review obligations as permission to execute</li>
      <li>Host or certify the AI provider</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Request and Action Inputs</h2>

    <p>Integration requires explicit inputs, including as applicable:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Actor identity (user, system, service)</li>
      <li>Signed request metadata</li>
      <li>Workflow identity and requested or declared action</li>
      <li>Source-system trust and approved-pathway status</li>
      <li>Deterministic context tags</li>
      <li>Request IDs, timestamps, and freshness windows</li>
      <li>Provider-result binding / correlation context</li>
      <li>Attributes required by the Compliance Playbook</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Missing or malformed required inputs result in fail-closed <strong>DENY</strong>.</p>

    <h2>Release Distribution and Activation</h2>

    <p>
      Signed Immutable Release Artifacts are distributed through Controlled Release
      Distribution. Before use:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Signatures and integrity are verified (Release Verification and Activation)</li>
      <li>Only an <strong>Active Verified Release</strong> governs runtime evaluation</li>
      <li>release_identity is fixed for each evaluation and recorded in evidence</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Draft playbooks and unsigned artifacts must not govern evaluation.</p>

    <h2>Evidence Integration</h2>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard emits Verifiable Decision Records per evaluation, including
      decision outcome, reason codes, release_identity, playbook_identity,
      intended-action context where applicable, and check outcomes where
      applicable.
    </p>

    <p>
      Evidence is integrity-protected decision evidence designed for investigation
      replay and verification. Append-only retention is supported when configured
      with a required evidence provider. Customers own provider selection,
      retention, and operational monitoring. Investigation surfaces are accessed
      through BeaconGuard Verify (read-only).
    </p>

    <h2>Packaging Qualification</h2>

    <p>
      Windows and Linux controlled deployment paths are evaluated under
      controlled internal qualification, including Linux OCI-oriented packaging
      paths where supported by deployment documentation. Native Windows OCI is
      not claimed as a proven packaging path. Public package downloads and general
      installer distribution are not implied. Customer production deployment is
      not authorized as a universal product claim.
    </p>

    <h2>Failure Handling</h2>

    <p>
      Applications must enforce <strong>DENY</strong> outcomes and must not proceed with
      provider invocation or enterprise effect on admission or action evaluation
      failure.
    </p>

    <p>Failure cases include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Missing or unverifiable Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>Trust, freshness, or replay check failures</li>
      <li>Input normalization errors</li>
      <li>Internal evaluation faults</li>
      <li>Absent required approvals (<code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code> and similar reason codes)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      All blocking cases return deterministic <strong>DENY</strong>. Human review via a
      Verifiable Decision Record obligation does not permit execution.
    </p>

    <h2>Environment Separation</h2>

    <p>
      Recommended environments include development, testing / validation, and
      production. Release artifacts and signing keys must not be casually shared
      across environments.
    </p>

